 [{:title
   "'thinking...' indicator is cleared by run-loop's own :agent_start event ~instantly, so it never stays visible during the actual model wait",
   :file "packages/eta-mu/src/cljs/eta_mu/infra/cli/tui_repl.cljs",
   :detail
   "The card's delivered-behavior claim is that the indicator 'shows between submitting a prompt and the first token/tool result'. It doesn't. tui_repl.cljs:120 renders [\"thinking...\"] and then immediately invokes loop/run-loop (line 122) with no intervening await. run-loop's first action is to emit :agent_start and :turn_start (packages/turn-processor/src/cljs/eta_mu/turn_processor/infra/loop.cljs:187-188) — synchronously at entry, before convert-to-llm and before stream-fn/first-token latency. tui-emit's guard (tui_repl.cljs:51-52) clears the indicator on ANY event, so :agent_start wipes it microseconds after it was drawn; the 'thinking...' bytes and the \\r+ESC[K clear land in the same stdout batch. On a real model with multi-second time-to-first-token the user sees no indicator at all during the wait — the feature is effectively dead for its stated purpose. The pty verification on the card can't distinguish this (a mock SSE server responds instantly), and the eta-mu test can't catch it either: FakeTerminal's clear-line is a no-op (tui_repl_test.cljs:14), so the test only proves 'thinking...' was written to the buffer, not that it survived until first output. Fix direction: clear only on output-producing events (message_update with text, tool_execution_start, tool-result message_end, turn_end), not on run-loop bookkeeping events.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "differential-render-host",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives true,
   :reasons
   ["Claim verified against the code; finding survives. (1) tui_repl.cljs:120 renders [\"thinking...\"] via host/render! and line 122 immediately awaits loop/run-loop with no intervening await. (2) run-loop's first statements (loop.cljs:187-188) are (emit! emit {:type :agent_start}) and (emit! emit {:type :turn_start}) — before convert-to-llm (line 202) and before stream-fn/first-token latency (line 206). (3) tui-emit's sink (tui_repl.cljs:50-52) clears the indicator via (host/render! status-state term []) on ANY event when the frame is non-empty, with no event-type discrimination, so :agent_start erases the indicator microseconds after it was drawn; the multi-second model wait then runs with nothing on screen. (4) The escape hatches don't apply: it is not grandfathered code — the indicator, the host, and the guard were all introduced in commit 054dd55, this card's own work, and the card itself lists the indicator as delivered scope ('What is delivered and real, not a stub'). It is not a documented accepted tradeoff — the card claims the indicator 'shows between submitting a prompt and the first token/tool result', which is false as delivered; the 'cleared ... the instant any event arrives' sentence describes the mechanism under the author's evident mistaken assumption that the first event is the first output event, and no card comment acknowledges that run-loop's bookkeeping events fire first. The card's own verification is genuinely blind to the defect, exactly as the reviewer states: the pty check used an instant mock SSE server, so \\r+ESC[K lands immediately before the reply text under both the buggy and intended behavior (indistinguishable), and the new unit test's FakeTerminal has a no-op clear-line (tui_repl_test.cljs:14) so 'thinking...' persists in the accumulated buffer regardless of when it would have been erased. (5) Not too minor: the indicator's sole stated purpose is visibility during the model wait, and it is never visible there on a real model — the card's scope-note delivered-behavior claim is untrue, which legitimately gates a review→done promotion. The suggested fix (clear only on output-producing events) is small and correct."
    "Verified against source: tui_repl.cljs:120 renders [\"thinking...\"] then immediately awaits loop/run-loop (line 122); run-loop emits :agent_start/:turn_start synchronously at entry (loop.cljs:187-188), before convert-to-llm (202) and before stream-fn (206) — i.e. before any model latency. tui-emit's guard (tui_repl.cljs:51-52) clears the indicator on ANY event whenever the frame is non-empty, and host/render! does store the frame (host.cljs:53), so :agent_start wipes the indicator microseconds after it is drawn. The indicator is therefore never visible during the actual time-to-first-token wait, directly contradicting the card's delivered-behavior claim (\"shows between submitting a prompt and the first token/tool result\") and the tui-emit docstring itself (\"shown while waiting for the model's first token or tool result\"). Not a documented tradeoff: no card comment or ledger entry accepts this; the scope note asserts the behavior as real delivered work. In scope: the indicator wiring is explicitly listed under the card's 'What is delivered and real, not a stub'. Not too minor: the card's verification (instant mock SSE) and the new test (FakeTerminal clear-line is a no-op at tui_repl_test.cljs:14) cannot and do not demonstrate the indicator persists through the wait, so the delivered claim is materially overstated and should be fixed (clear only on output-producing events) before promotion to done."]}],
 :all
 [{:title
   "Law-gate throw from record-turn! escapes turn error handling — one empty assistant reply kills the interactive REPL (and misreports single-turn success as exit 1)",
   :file "packages/eta-mu/src/cljs/eta_mu/infra/session.cljs",
   :detail
   "write-artifact! is law-gated (infra/session.cljs:40-42, added in the previous review round): any artifact that fails law.session/valid-artifact? throws 'Refusing to persist an invalid session artifact: <malli explain>'. All three success-path callers invoke record-turn! OUTSIDE the try/catch that guards loop/run-loop: repl.cljs:130 (try only wraps lines 124-128), tui_repl.cljs:128 (try only wraps 121-126), agent.cljs:48 (try only wraps 43-47). Concrete reachable trigger: an assistant message with :content []. The turn-processor law requires [:content [:vector {:min 1} ...]] (packages/turn-processor/src/cljs/eta_mu/turn_processor/law/message.cljs:69), but the OpenAI client produces :content [] whenever a completion carries no text and no tool calls — accumulator->response omits :content when the accumulated text is \"\" (extern/openai.cljs:162-169), so openai-response->assistant-message yields (vec (concat [] [])) = [] (turn-processor/shape/message.cljs:151-154). An OpenAI-compatible server that streams only {\"delta\":{},\"finish_reason\":\"stop\"} (null/empty content — happens with local servers, refusal/content-filter responses) therefore: (a) in the REPL/TUI, unwinds the entire input loop and exits the process 1 with a raw Malli explain dump — a single odd provider reply terminates the whole interactive session, while every genuine run-loop failure is caught and the session continues; (b) in single-turn/piped mode, the turn already succeeded and streamed to stdout, yet the process exits 1 reporting a schema error, misreporting a successful turn as a failure. The same crash class applies to /clear (repl.cljs:118, tui_repl.cljs:114) and to any mid-session fs error (disk full), though those are less likely triggers. No test or e2e covers an invalid-but-produced message reaching the law gate: all mock servers send non-empty content, and the new tests only assert the happy-path append. Fix options: wrap the record-turn!/clear! calls in the REPLs so persistence failure logs and continues (conversation survives), or make the shape layer guarantee law-valid messages (e.g. emit a placeholder text part for empty completions).",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "session-persistence",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["Every checkable element of the claim verifies against the actual code. (1) The law really does reject empty content: turn-processor law/message.cljs:69 requires assistant [:content [:vector {:min 1} assistant-content-schema]], and eta-mu law/session.cljs:27 embeds message/agent-message-schema in the artifact, so an assistant message with :content [] fails law/valid-artifact?. (2) The empty message is genuinely producible: extern/openai.cljs accumulator->response (lines 162-169) omits :content when accumulated text is \"\" and no tool calls exist, and shape/message.cljs openai-response->assistant-message (lines 151-154) then yields (vec (concat [] [])) = []; a chunk of {\"delta\":{},\"finish_reason\":\"stop\"} or null content takes exactly this path through apply-chunk! without error. (3) run-loop (infra/loop.cljs:207-209, 238-240) performs no law validation and returns the invalid assistant message as a successful turn, so the REPL try/catch (which only wraps loop/run-loop) never fires. (4) record-turn! sits outside that try in all three hosts at the exact cited lines — repl.cljs:130, tui_repl.cljs:128, agent.cljs:48 — and run-repl's outer try has only a finally, so the write-artifact! throw (session.cljs:40-42) unwinds the whole input loop into agent.cljs handle's catch, printing a raw Malli explain and exiting 1; in single-turn/piped mode the turn already streamed successfully yet the process exits 1. (5) Not grandfathered: infra/session.cljs and law/session.cljs are untracked new files and repl.cljs is modified in this card's uncommitted working tree; the law-gate was added in this card's own previous review round. (6) Not documented as accepted: card comment 69's accepted-with-rationale list covers eager create!, js/Date precedent, per-run overrides, and concurrency — never the law-gate-throw-escapes-the-REPL interaction. (7) Not minor: a single refusal/content-filter/local-server empty reply kills the entire interactive session while genuine run-loop failures are caught and survived — a disproportionate crash in the card's core delivered path (persistence in the turn loop), with zero test coverage of an invalid-but-produced message reaching the gate. The finding is real, in-scope, and promotion-gating."]}
  {:title
   "extern.os/homedir treats HOME=\"\" as set, yielding a cwd-relative '.eta-mu' sessions dir",
   :file "packages/eta-mu/src/cljs/eta_mu/extern/os.cljs",
   :detail
   "Line 9: (or (process/env \"HOME\") (.homedir os)). process/env returns the raw value whenever the var is set (extern/process.cljs:17 — when-let passes \"\"), and \"\" is truthy in ClojureScript, so HOME=\"\" resolves to \"\" and eta-mu-home becomes (path/join \"\" \".eta-mu\") — a directory relative to the current working directory. Sessions are then silently written into <cwd>/.eta-mu/sessions and are invisible to 'eta-mu session' run from any other directory. This is the twin of the ETA_MU_HOME='' case the previous review explicitly fixed via the (seq override) guard (infra/session.cljs:24-27); the new extern.os boundary reintroduces the same edge unhandled. Rare (set-but-empty HOME in minimal containers), but the repo has already legislated this class of bug once.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "session-persistence",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The code reading is accurate: extern/os.cljs:9 `(or (process/env \"HOME\") (.homedir os))` does pass \"\" through (when-let in extern/process.cljs:17 treats \"\" as set, and \"\" is truthy in CLJS), so HOME=\"\" would yield a cwd-relative `.eta-mu` dir. However this does not gate a review→done promotion. (1) The finding is self-labeled a nit and admits the scenario is rare (set-but-empty HOME only occurs in pathological minimal containers; every real platform either sets HOME to a valid path or unsets it, in which case .homedir os resolves correctly). (2) The card's documented legislation (comment 2026-07-16 code review) scoped the empty-string guard to ETA_MU_HOME specifically — the test/e2e isolation hook the card itself introduced and therefore owned. HOME is process-inherited environment, not a knob this card delivered; the `(seq override)` guard was a fix for the card's own new override, not a repo-wide policy that every env lookup must empty-check. (3) Failure mode is graceful degradation, not corruption: sessions are written under <cwd>/.eta-mu and remain readable from that cwd; nothing crashes and no data is lost. (4) Severity: nit-level consistency suggestion at an extern boundary, comparable to the nits the prior review explicitly 'accepted with rationale' rather than fixed. A one-line `(or (some-> (process/env \"HOME\") not-empty) (.homedir os))` hardening is reasonable follow-up polish, but per skeptic rules a finding must be promotion-gating to survive, and this one is not."
    "The mechanism described is real — extern/os.cljs:9 `(or (process/env \"HOME\") (.homedir os))` does pass \"\" through (process/env's when-let returns \"\" since it's truthy in CLJS), so HOME=\"\" would yield a cwd-relative `.eta-mu` dir, and the card's own review did fix the twin ETA_MU_HOME='' case via the (seq override) guard at infra/session.cljs:24-27. However, this finding does not gate a review→done promotion: (1) it is self-classified as a nit, and the card's review history establishes the taxonomy — 'no blockers, 2 should-fix' gated the review, while nits were either folded in opportunistically or explicitly accepted with rationale (eager create!, js/Date in domain, per-run overrides, whole-file-rewrite concurrency all accepted un-fixed); (2) the trigger is a pathological set-but-empty HOME, rarer than the ETA_MU_HOME='' case that was fixed because empty-string overrides are plausible in test/e2e scripts (ETA_MU_HOME is the documented test-isolation hook — scripts genuinely pass empty values; nobody exports HOME=\"\" in practice, and when HOME is absent it is unset, which the fallback handles correctly); (3) even in the failure mode, session writes still succeed and remain consistent within the cwd — degraded placement, not data loss or a broken DoD item; all of the card's DoD gates (tests, e2e, kondo) are green. A one-line `(not-empty)` guard at the os boundary would be a reasonable follow-up nit for symmetry with the ETA_MU_HOME guard, but under the card's own precedent this class of item is nit-level, and nit-level items do not gate promotion."]}
  {:title
   "session list preview can split a UTF-16 surrogate pair and embeds raw newlines from piped prompts",
   :file "packages/eta-mu/src/cljs/eta_mu/domain/session.cljs",
   :detail
   "summary (line 84) truncates with (subs preview 0 (min (count preview) 60)). subs counts UTF-16 code units, so a 60-unit cut through an emoji/wide char leaves a lone surrogate that prints as U+FFFD in every 'eta-mu session' row. Separately, run-piped-input stores the entire trimmed stdin (including newlines) as the user message content (repl.cljs:157-160), so the first-user preview can contain literal newlines, breaking the one-line-per-session output of commands/sessions.cljs:19-23. Display-only corruption; no data loss.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "session-persistence",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The claim is technically accurate but fails the promotion-gating test. Verified against the code: domain/session.cljs:84 does `(subs preview 0 (min (count preview) 60))`, and in ClojureScript subs/count operate on UTF-16 code units, so a non-BMP character straddling the 60-unit boundary would indeed yield a lone surrogate rendering as U+FFFD — but only in the narrow edge case where such a character sits exactly at the cut point. The newline part is also real: repl.cljs:157 `(str/trim raw)` strips only leading/trailing whitespace, so multi-line piped stdin retains interior newlines that flow into the first-user preview printed raw by commands/sessions.cljs:19-23. However, both files are brand-new, untracked code delivered by this card, and the card's Definition of Done is fully met (artifacts written and listed, resume verified e2e, law schema, all gates green: 135 tests/266 assertions, kondo 0/0, e2e 4/47). The reviewer self-labels this a 'nit' and concedes 'display-only corruption; no data loss' — session ids, timestamps, models, and counts all print correctly, the listing remains usable, and the persisted artifacts are untouched. A cosmetic truncation polish (e.g., code-point-aware subs + newline-to-space normalization in summary) is appropriate follow-up work, not a gate on a review→done promotion. Per the skeptic mandate that surviving findings must be real, in-scope, AND promotion-gating, this nit does not gate."
    "The mechanics are factually accurate — domain/session.cljs:84 does `(subs preview 0 (min (count preview) 60))` and CLJS subs indexes UTF-16 code units, so an astral-plane char straddling the 60-unit boundary would leave a lone surrogate rendering as U+FFFD; and repl.cljs:157-159 `str/trim`s piped stdin but preserves interior newlines, which would then appear literally inside the quoted preview printed by commands/sessions.cljs:19-23, breaking the one-line-per-row format. However, this is refuted as promotion-gating on three grounds. (1) The finding itself is filed as severity 'nit' and concedes 'display-only corruption; no data loss' — the skeptic charter says findings too minor to gate a review→done promotion are refuted, and a cosmetic truncation artifact in a listing preview is precisely that class. (2) Both triggers are edge cases: surrogate splitting requires an emoji/wide char landing exactly on code-unit 60 of the first user message, and newline embedding requires a multi-line piped prompt (interactive readline input cannot contain newlines), with the artifact data itself remaining intact and `session show` unaffected. (3) The card's delivered scope (per the 2026-07-16 implementation record) is persist/resume/list with law-gated artifacts; fixed-width preview truncation is a conventional CLI projection decision of the same kind the review already accepted elsewhere on this card (e.g., whole-file rewrite without locking, eager create! write). This is legitimate follow-up hardening (e.g., strip/escape \\n and cut on code points) but does not block promotion."]}
  {:title
   "Sibling-package session schemas not noted in the card's anomaly log",
   :file "packages/eta-mu/src/cljs/eta_mu/law/session.cljs",
   :detail
   "The constitution's anomaly rule (AGENTS.md, Construction Order) requires that shapes discovered 'in this package or a sibling' be described in the anomaly log. The card logs one anomaly (the 'session' command-name collision) but not the pre-existing sibling session shapes: packages/runtime/src/cljs/eta_mu/runtime/law/session.cljs (session-context-schema: int timestamps, :active-tool-names, :metadata), packages/runtime/src/cljs/eta_mu/runtime/domain/session.cljs, and packages/runtime/src/cljs/eta_mu/coding/law/session.cljs. These do not contradict the new EDN artifact (different package, different concept — runtime host session context vs persisted agent transcript), so they don't invalidate the work, but the monorepo now has two divergent session-schema families and the legislated anomaly log is silent on it. Everything else in the constitution lens checks out and was re-verified in the working tree: I/O is confined to extern.* (infra/session.cljs uses only extern.fs/os/process/path; domain/session.cljs and law/session.cljs are pure, matching the domain/receipt.cljs precedent already adjudicated on the card); the law is enforced at both boundaries (write-artifact! refuses invalid artifacts at infra/session.cljs:40-42, load-artifact validates reads at :74-76, and the router's law.command validate! covers the new 'session' entry); no utils namespaces; no core.async or .then chains (the js/Promise. constructor at repl.cljs:140 matches the extern/readline.cljs:39 event-boundary precedent); gates re-run green: kondo 0 errors/0 warnings, 135 tests/266 assertions, e2e 4 tests/47 assertions.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "session-persistence",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The claim is factually accurate — packages/runtime/src/cljs/eta_mu/runtime/law/session.cljs (session-context-schema), runtime/domain/session.cljs, and coding/law/session.cljs do exist, and the card's 2026-07-16 implementation comment logs only the 'session' command-name anomaly — but the finding fails on every gating axis. (1) The reviewer self-classifies it as a 'nit' and explicitly concedes the sibling shapes do not contradict or invalidate the delivered work; a documentation-completeness nitpick about a card comment cannot gate a review→done promotion. (2) The anomaly rule applies to 'shapes in play' for the card's targeted projections: this card's projection is the persisted agent-session transcript artifact (law.session SessionArtifact), whereas the runtime session-context-schema is a different concept in a different package (in-memory runtime host context with int timestamps and :active-tool-names, not a persisted EDN artifact). Discovering a same-named but semantically unrelated schema in a sibling package is not an anomaly that bears on the card's shapes — there is nothing to reconcile, and the reviewer agrees ('different package, different concept'). (3) The card already carries a thorough discovery/anomaly record (command-name collision, ETA_MU_HOME isolation hook, /clear semantics, resume override semantics, concurrency tradeoff) and a completed review pass documenting adjudicated nits with rationale; all gates are re-verified green (135 tests/266 assertions, kondo 0/0, e2e 4/47). Even if one accepted the strictest reading of the anomaly rule, the remedy is appending one sentence to a card comment — cosmetic, not promotion-gating."
    "The narrow factual point holds (the sibling files exist — packages/runtime/src/cljs/eta_mu/runtime/law/session.cljs, runtime/domain/session.cljs, coding/law/session.cljs — and the card's anomaly log records only the 'session' command-name collision), but the finding fails every gate for survival. (1) It is self-labeled 'nit' and the reviewer concedes the sibling shapes 'do not contradict the new EDN artifact... so they don't invalidate the work' — a non-invalidating observation about log completeness cannot gate a review→done promotion. (2) The anomaly rule's mandatory trigger is a discovery that 'invalidates the shape of your targeted projections' (stop and re-describe); the describe-and-keep-going branch covers anomalies actually encountered in the discovery path of the work, and the runtime session-context schema (int timestamps, :active-tool-names — a live host-session context, not a persisted transcript) is a different concept in a different package that the card's slices never touched. (3) Those sibling files are grandfathered code — committed in 11f31e1 before this card's work began (the card's own law/session.cljs is still uncommitted) — so their existence is not a finding against this card. (4) The card's delivered scope and DoD contain no cross-package schema-census item; every DoD checkbox is verified in the card's adjudicated review comment (135 tests/266 assertions, kondo 0/0, e2e 4/47, all green), and the review already closed with accepted-tradeoff rationale documented. Appending one line to a comment thread is always possible post-promotion; withholding promotion over it would be process theater, not contract enforcement."]}
